## Configure the LiveKit Twilio connector

This project uses the `livekit/connector/{{.Telephony.Carrier}}` route: the
generated `telephony_bridge.py` speaks Twilio Media Streams over a WebSocket
and joins a local LiveKit room, where the agent worker handles the call. There
is no SIP trunk and no Redis. A normal HTTPS tunnel is enough, because Twilio
only needs to reach the bridge over HTTPS and WSS. This is our own open-source
bridge; LiveKit's hosted connector is Cloud only.

### Required configuration and credentials

Set these in `.env` or your deployment secret store:

- `{{.Telephony.AccountSIDEnv}}` and `{{.Telephony.AuthTokenEnv}}`: your Twilio
  Account SID and Auth Token (Twilio Console account dashboard). The Auth Token
  also validates inbound webhook signatures.
- `{{.Telephony.FromNumberEnv}}`: your Voice-capable Twilio number, E.164.
- `LIVEKIT_URL`, `LIVEKIT_API_KEY`, `LIVEKIT_API_SECRET`: your self-hosted
  LiveKit Server. The bridge and worker connect out to it, so it needs no
  public SIP or RTP. Local `unmute dev --telephony` supplies its own dev pair.
- `UNMUTE_PUBLIC_URL`: the public HTTPS origin the carrier reaches; local
  `unmute dev --telephony` supplies a managed tunnel.
{{- if .Telephony.HasOutbound}}
- `UNMUTE_OUTBOUND_TOKEN`: the bearer token that authorizes
  `POST /telephony/outbound`; local `unmute dev --telephony` mints it.
{{- end}}

### Run it

Point the Twilio number's voice webhook at `POST /telephony/inbound` (the dev
command sets it automatically and prints the previous value).
{{- if .Telephony.HasOutbound}} Place an outbound call with
`unmute dev --telephony --to <E.164>`, or POST `{"to": "<E.164>"}` with the
bearer token to `/telephony/outbound` from your own application.
{{- end}}

### Scale the route

The bridge and worker are stateless: run more replicas behind one number. Each
call is one LiveKit room, and the join token dispatches the worker into it.
{{- else}}
## Configure self-hosted LiveKit SIP

This project uses the `livekit/sip/{{.Telephony.Carrier}}` route. Unmute emits
the selected worker and SIP setup inputs. The generated Compose file starts
local LiveKit, Redis, and LiveKit SIP services for development; it does not
provision production infrastructure or carrier resources. This route was
checked against the official web documentation on July 20, 2026, but not
through the LiveKit docs MCP server.

### Required configuration and credentials

Set these values in `.env` or your deployment secret store:

- `LIVEKIT_URL`: the WebSocket URL of your self-hosted LiveKit Server.
- `LIVEKIT_API_KEY` and `LIVEKIT_API_SECRET`: a key pair from the `keys`
  section of that server's configuration. LiveKit SIP uses the same pair.
- `REDIS_URL`: the Redis deployment shared by LiveKit Server and LiveKit SIP.
- `{{.Telephony.SIPAddressEnv}}`, `{{.Telephony.SIPUsernameEnv}}`, and
  `{{.Telephony.SIPPasswordEnv}}`: {{.Telephony.CredentialHint}}.
- `{{.Telephony.FromNumberEnv}}`: the phone number linked to that SIP trunk.
{{if .Telephony.HasInbound}}- `LIVEKIT_SIP_INBOUND_TRUNK`: the ID printed by
  `lk sip inbound create`.
{{end}}{{if or .Telephony.HasOutbound .Telephony.HasWarm}}- `LIVEKIT_SIP_OUTBOUND_TRUNK`: the ID printed by
  `lk sip outbound create`.
{{end}}
Use the
[{{.Telephony.Carrier}} provider guide]({{.Telephony.ProviderDocs}}) for the
carrier-side trunk fields. A normal HTTPS tunnel doesn't expose SIP signaling
or RTP. Generated local Compose defaults to SIP port `5060` and UDP RTP ports
`10000-10100`; set `UNMUTE_LIVEKIT_SIP_PORT` and
`UNMUTE_LIVEKIT_RTP_PORT_RANGE` when those host ports are unavailable. Set
`UNMUTE_LIVEKIT_PORT` to move the host-side LiveKit API port from `7880`.

### Create the LiveKit SIP resources

Materialize the generated JSON before passing it to `lk`. The committed inputs
contain variable names only; the `/tmp` copies contain resolved configuration
and must not be committed. Export the values in your shell first;
`envsubst` doesn't read `.env` itself.

{{if .Telephony.HasInbound}}
```sh
envsubst < sip-inbound-trunk.json > /tmp/unmute-sip-inbound-trunk.json
lk sip inbound create /tmp/unmute-sip-inbound-trunk.json
# Set LIVEKIT_SIP_INBOUND_TRUNK to the returned SIPTrunkID.

envsubst < sip-dispatch-rule.json > /tmp/unmute-sip-dispatch-rule.json
lk sip dispatch create /tmp/unmute-sip-dispatch-rule.json
```

Point the carrier's origination URI at your public LiveKit SIP endpoint (the
address you deployed LiveKit SIP on). The dispatch rule creates one `call-*`
room per inbound call and dispatches the `{{.AgentName}}` worker with
`direction: inbound` metadata.
{{end}}
{{if or .Telephony.HasOutbound .Telephony.HasWarm}}
```sh
envsubst < sip-outbound-trunk.json > /tmp/unmute-sip-outbound-trunk.json
lk sip outbound create /tmp/unmute-sip-outbound-trunk.json \
  --auth-user "${{.Telephony.SIPUsernameEnv}}" \
  --auth-pass "${{.Telephony.SIPPasswordEnv}}"
# Set LIVEKIT_SIP_OUTBOUND_TRUNK to the returned SIPTrunkID.
```

For an outbound call, dispatch this agent to a new room with JSON job metadata
in this shape:

```json
{
  "direction": "outbound",
  "phone_number": "+14155550123",
  "call_start": {}
}
```

The worker validates the E.164 destination and authored `call_start` fields
before dialing. It uses the stored outbound trunk for outbound calls and warm
transfers.
{{end}}

### Scale the route

Redis is control-plane infrastructure, not an audio hop. LiveKit Server uses
it for distributed room state and routing; LiveKit SIP uses the same Redis
deployment to coordinate SIP participants with the server. Audio still flows
through the SIP/RTP and LiveKit media services. Scale worker replicas on active
jobs, keep the shared Redis available, and drain active calls before shutdown.
{{- end}}
